in the wake of their commissioner being  forced to stand down the sarah everard  murder by a serving officer allegations  of sexism and homophobia at charing  cross police station the criticism for  the uk's biggest police force keeps  coming  no one was convicted of daniel morgan's  murder 35 years ago but the alleged  corruption that surrounded the case led  to decades of scrutiny of london's  police and today the police inspectorate  found after 35 years the lessons have  not been learned  the key finding is that they're just not  taking it seriously enough  there is too much that goes wrong with  some pretty basic procedures  that we don't think the met are doing  all they can to minimize the risk of  corruption getting a hold in the force  as it has in the past  the inspectorate found in the last two  years the met has recruited more than  100 people who've committed offences and  failed to properly supervise these  people hundreds of items such as drugs  cash and exhibits are missing with the  policies for keeping them safe branded  as dire and there's a failed oversight  procedure for vetting those in sensitive  posts such as child protection  there's no doubt it's a hard-hitting  report and speaking personally it's a  difficult read at times um  professionally i find some of the issues  a little bit embarrassing  i take some comfort in the positivity  you know they've come down on the side  to say that we're not we're not  institutionally corrupt as an  organization which is a positive  i think they talk about advances we've  made in our homicide investigation and  and areas of vetting as well so there  are positives in there but we must we  must also be open to the fact that  there's a lot of criticism in there  daniel morgan was found dead in 1987 in  the car park of the golden lion pub here  in south london  what happened at this pub in sydney in  south london 35 years ago led to six  police investigations an inquest and an  eight-year public inquiry which  concluded last year that the  metropolitan police was institutionally  corrupt  today the inspectorate concluded the met  was not institutionally corrupt however  procedures for rooting out corrupt staff  were fundamentally flawed  daniel morgan's family who is suing the  met claimed a culture of corruption  continues  if you look at the way  the attitude that they have towards  corruption as displayed in this report  now that to me speaks since it says  institutional corruption  a completely nonchalant lackadaisical  approach to uh  police corruption and i would say that  there are cover-ups and you know the old  blue  line is closing somewhere every day  there were improvements in some areas  but not enough to make this report happy  reading for whoever takes over as the  new commissioner  jason farrell sky news
 

